2.5  Speculative execution and CPU flaws

TL;DR
  - To stay busy, a CPU guesses: which way a branch goes, what memory will hold,
    and runs ahead. Wrong guesses are thrown away.
  - Thrown away, but not without a trace: the caches keep what was read.
    Spectre, Meltdown and their successors (2018 onwards) use that to read
    memory a program should never see.
  - The kernel works around each flaw it knows (mitigations), helped by
    microcode updates. It lists them per flaw.

      grep -r . /sys/devices/system/cpu/vulnerabilities/
      journalctl -k | grep -i microcode    # microcode loaded at boot

On your machine
  Mitigated gather_data_sampling: Microcode
            spec_store_bypass: Speculative Store Bypass disabled via prctl
            spectre_v1: usercopy/swapgs barriers and __user pointer sanitization
            spectre_v2: Enhanced / Automatic IBRS; IBPB: conditional; RSB
            filling; PBRSB-eIBRS: SW sequence; BHI: SW loop, KVM: SW loop
  Immune    10 others: this CPU doesn't have them

Pitfalls
  - Keep the microcode package installed and updated (intel-microcode, amd-ucode
    or amd64-microcode): many fixes need it.
  - mitigations=off on the kernel command line buys some speed and drops every
    protection: only for isolated machines running trusted code.
  - "Vulnerable" for one entry isn't always an alarm: some protections are only
    applied to programs that ask for them (spec_store_bypass is often left to
    prctl or seccomp).
  - Mitigations cost most on older CPUs and system-call-heavy work; newer CPUs
    fix several flaws in hardware.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 2.5 --why

Related
  Commands  grep, journalctl
  Paths     /sys/devices/system/cpu/vulnerabilities/
  Hub       idk cpu CPU
  See also  idk 2.1 What a CPU does: instructions and architectures
  See also  idk 14.2 The kernel command line
